## Verification

The test suite is intentionally adversarial. It does not inspect screenshots; it asserts the mathematical invariant.

Run:

```bash
cd ~/hiz-occlusion
node --test
```

Verified cases:

| Test | What it proves |
|---|---|
| `pyramid is exact and covers NPOT targets (reversed-Z)` | Ceil-sized mip chain covers every pixel on sizes `1x1` through `31x17`; every parent equals the min over its exact base interval. |
| `pyramid is exact and covers NPOT targets (classic depth)` | Same for classic `max` reduction. |
| `reversed-Z culls only geometry behind the farthest occluder` | AABB behind `0.9` culls; AABB in front does not. |
| `thin occluders and depth-discontinuous silhouettes stay conservative` | AABB fully inside a thin near occluder culls; an AABB that straddles the silhouette into far background does not. |
| `NPOT edge pixel is not dropped by mip truncation` | A far edge pixel on `7x5` prevents a false cull; the candidate that avoids it culls. |
| `OpenGL-style clip depth (-1..1) is converted before culling` | `depthRange: '-1..1'` is converted to raw depth consistently. |
| `previous-screen border is invalidated under camera motion` | Left/right/top/bottom border crossings return `previous-screen-border`; a full-viewport footprint still culls. |
| `near and far extremes do not create false positives` | `0`, `1`, and coplanar cases are handled conservatively. |
| `perspective reprojection culls behind planes and rejects behind-camera AABBs` | Perspective divide and `w <= 0` handling are correct. |
| `fuzz: no culled AABB contains a visible base sample` | 2,500 random NPOT depth buffers and AABBs. For every `occluded === true`, every base sample in the query rectangle is strictly farther than the AABB's nearest point. |

The fuzz test is the actual proof of the conservativeness invariant:

```text
if (result.occluded) {
  for every pixel in result.pixelRect:
    reversed-Z:  projected.nearDepth < depth[pixel]
    classic:     projected.farDepth  > depth[pixel]
}
```

If this ever failed, the culler would have culled visible geometry. It passes for both depth conventions and all tested NPOT sizes.
